RuleBasedBreakIterator::clone() (what ubrk_clone / ubrk_safeClone call) crashes
instead of reporting U_MEMORY_ALLOCATION_ERROR when any allocation other than the
iterator object itself fails. The copy constructor delegates to the default
constructor, which allocates fDictionaryCache, fBreakCache and their UVector32
buffers (64, 32, 832 and 32 bytes); if one of those fails it records the error in
fErrorCode and leaves the pointers null, and the copy constructor then runs
operator=, which ends with fBreakCache->reset(). operator= also allocates
fLookAheadMatches without checking the result, and handleNext() stores into it
whenever a look-ahead rule matches (the grapheme rules have one, for regional
indicator sequences), so that failure crashes on the first iteration instead.

JSC clones a break iterator for every Intl.Segmenter.prototype.segment() call and
every %Segments.prototype%[Symbol.iterator]() call and checks the status, but the
crash happens inside ubrk_clone before there is a status to check. This is the
crash Bun's crash reporter shows on Windows as
RuleBasedBreakIterator::BreakCache::reset <- RuleBasedBreakIterator::operator=
<- RuleBasedBreakIterator::clone <- ubrk_clone <- JSC::IntlSegmenter::segment,
where the static CRT's malloc returns null once the process is out of commit.

operator= now refuses to touch an iterator whose construction failed, records a
failed fLookAheadMatches allocation in fErrorCode, and clone() (plus the
rules-string constructor, the other in-tree user of operator=) reports a copy
with a failed fErrorCode as a failed clone, which the C API already maps to
U_MEMORY_ALLOCATION_ERROR. Successful clones are unchanged.

Present in ICU 73.2 and 78.3 and still in ICU main at the time of writing.

--- a/source/common/rbbi.cpp
+++ b/source/common/rbbi.cpp
@@ -179,6 +179,9 @@
     if (U_SUCCESS(status)) {
         *this = *bi;
         delete bi;
+        if (U_FAILURE(fErrorCode)) {
+            status = fErrorCode;
+        }
     }
 }
 
@@ -281,6 +284,15 @@
     if (this == &that) {
         return *this;
     }
+    if (U_FAILURE(fErrorCode)) {
+        // Construction failed (RuleBasedBreakIterator(UErrorCode*) could not
+        // allocate fBreakCache / fDictionaryCache, which are dereferenced below)
+        // or an earlier assignment did; the iterator is unusable either way.
+        // The copy constructor gets here through the default constructor, which
+        // has no status to report that through, so clone() and the rules
+        // constructor check fErrorCode afterwards instead.
+        return *this;
+    }
     BreakIterator::operator=(that);
 
     if (fLanguageBreakEngines != nullptr) {
@@ -320,6 +332,11 @@
     if (fData && fData->fForwardTable->fLookAheadResultsSize > 0) {
         fLookAheadMatches = static_cast<int32_t *>(
             uprv_malloc(fData->fForwardTable->fLookAheadResultsSize * sizeof(int32_t)));
+        if (fLookAheadMatches == nullptr) {
+            // handleNext() stores into this buffer unconditionally whenever a
+            // look-ahead rule matches.
+            fErrorCode = U_MEMORY_ALLOCATION_ERROR;
+        }
     }
 
 
@@ -346,7 +363,15 @@
 //-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 RuleBasedBreakIterator*
 RuleBasedBreakIterator::clone() const {
-    return new RuleBasedBreakIterator(*this);
+    RuleBasedBreakIterator *result = new RuleBasedBreakIterator(*this);
+    if (result != nullptr && U_FAILURE(result->fErrorCode)) {
+        // The copy could not allocate its caches or look-ahead buffer; report
+        // that the same way as failing to allocate the object itself, which
+        // ubrk_clone() / ubrk_safeClone() turn into U_MEMORY_ALLOCATION_ERROR.
+        delete result;
+        return nullptr;
+    }
+    return result;
 }
 
